{
  "$schema": "https://turbo.build/schema.json",
  // `envMode` is unset, so turbo runs strict: a task's environment is ONLY
  // what is listed here (plus its own `env`). A variable an operator exports
  // and turbo does not forward does not fail — it silently reads as unset, and
  // the task behaves as if the operator had typed nothing. Two classes live
  // here, and both are load-bearing:
  //
  //   - boot secrets, for the `dev` task (`apps/api` runs the real server, so
  //     it does a real `getEnv()`), and `MODULE_CONTRACT_ENFORCE`, the boot
  //     policy `apps/api/src/lib/modules/module-loader.ts` reads the same way;
  //   - the `warn|fail|off` knobs of the root `verify:*` scripts, documented in
  //     their own headers as the supported override. Without a passthrough,
  //     `MODULE_ISOLATION_POLICY=warn bun run check` did exactly nothing —
  //     turbo dropped the variable and the script kept its `fail` default.
  //
  // `CONSUMER_DRIFT_POLICY` is deliberately absent: `check-consumer-versions.ts`
  // runs from `publish-core.yml` directly, never through a turbo task.
  //
  // `CI` is deliberately absent too, and for the OPPOSITE reason — turbo already
  // forwards it. The two `verify:*` gates pin `POLICY = "fail"` under CI so a
  // green pipeline cannot be bought with `…_POLICY=off`, and that pin is only
  // worth anything if `CI` survives strict mode. Probed 2026-08-25, printing
  // `process.env` from inside the task: `CI=true APPSTRATE_PROBE_UNLISTED=yes
  // GITHUB_ACTIONS=true turbo run '//#verify:module-isolation' --force` yields
  // `CI="true" GITHUB_ACTIONS="true" RANDOM=undefined` — turbo drops the
  // unlisted variable and keeps `CI`, which is in its built-in system set.
  // Adding it here would be a no-op that implies the pin was broken.

  // ── globalDependencies ────────────────────────────────────────────────
  // Files outside every workspace whose content changes what a task VERIFIES.
  // Task `inputs` are workspace-relative, so a root file can only enter a
  // workspace task's hash from here.
  //
  // `tsconfig.base.json` is the whole of it, and it is not decorative: the 22
  // tsconfigs in this repo `extends` it, and it is where `strict`,
  // `noUncheckedIndexedAccess`, `noUnusedLocals`, `noImplicitOverride` and
  // `noFallthroughCasesInSwitch` are set. Measured before this entry existed: a
  // one-line edit flipping `"strict": true` → `false` moved NONE of the 21
  // `typecheck` hashes (`turbo run typecheck --dry=json`, byte-identical
  // output), so turbo printed `Cached: 21`, tsc never ran, and `bun run check`
  // reported green over a codebase whose type discipline had just been switched
  // off — with `.turbo/cache` restored across CI runs via `restore-keys`, that
  // green survives the merge. The CI cache key in
  // `.github/workflows/check.yml` hashes this file for the same reason.
  //
  // Two ROOT tasks (`//#typecheck:scripts`, `//#verify:type-coverage`) already
  // listed it in their own `inputs` — those resolve against the repo root, so
  // they could. This entry generalises that to the workspace tasks, and the two
  // local listings stay: harmless, and they document the dependency where the
  // task is defined.
  //
  // `bun.lock` is the second entry, and it is here rather than on any single
  // task because EVERY cached task reads `node_modules`: `tsc` resolves the
  // `.d.ts` of every dependency, `eslint` loads its plugin CODE from there,
  // `type-coverage` reads the same program `tsc` does, `vite build` bundles it.
  // A caret-range bump — a new `typescript-eslint` minor, a patched `@types/*` —
  // changes all of that while touching NO `package.json`, and a lock-only
  // change was measured replaying `//#lint` from cache: the new rules never
  // ran, and the gate reported the previous resolution's verdict. Turbo does
  // derive per-package external-dependency hashes from the lockfile, so this
  // entry overlaps that for some tasks; the overlap is a redundant
  // invalidation, and the alternative is a silent stale pass.
  //
  // Workspace tasks cannot list it themselves — task `inputs` are
  // workspace-relative and this is a root file, which is the same reason
  // `tsconfig.base.json` is here. `.github/workflows/check.yml` hashes it into
  // the restored-cache key already, which starts a fresh lineage across CI runs
  // but says nothing about a replay within one; this closes that half.
  //
  // Probe when changing it: `turbo run '//#lint' --dry=json`, EDIT `bun.lock`
  // (turbo hashes content — `touch` moves nothing), run again — the hash must
  // move.
  //
  // Probe when changing it: `turbo run typecheck --dry=json`, EDIT
  // tsconfig.base.json (turbo hashes content — `touch` moves nothing and reads
  // as a false negative), run again — every hash must move.
  "globalDependencies": ["tsconfig.base.json", "bun.lock"],
  "globalPassThroughEnv": [
    "BETTER_AUTH_SECRET",
    "CONNECTION_ENCRYPTION_KEY",
    "CONNECT_SESSION_SECRET",
    "UPLOAD_SIGNING_SECRET",
    "RUN_TOKEN_SECRET",
    "MODULE_CONTRACT_ENFORCE",
    "MODULE_ISOLATION_POLICY",
    "MODULE_CONTRACT_POLICY"
  ],
  "tasks": {
    // Two workspaces define `build`, and neither one builds only `src/**`:
    // `apps/web` runs `vite build`, which reads `index.html`, `vite.config.ts`
    // and `public/**`, and `apps/cli` runs `bun scripts/build.ts`. None of
    // those was an input, so editing the Vite config replayed a cached build
    // and left `dist/` stale — and `Dockerfile` runs `bun run build`, so the
    // stale `dist/` is what ships. Same rule as `typecheck` below: the inputs
    // must be what the command READS, not what the package is "about". Globs
    // that match nothing in a given workspace cost nothing.
    //
    // What these inputs deliberately do NOT list is the workspace dependencies
    // `apps/web` and `apps/cli` bundle (`@appstrate/ui`, `@appstrate/core`,
    // `@appstrate/module-chat`, `@appstrate/shared-types`, …), because
    // `dependsOn: ["^build"]` already carries them — including for the packages
    // that define NO `build` script. Turbo 2.10.8 still creates the task
    // (`--dry=json` shows it with `"command": "<NONEXISTENT>"`), still hashes it
    // over these same inputs resolved in that workspace, and still folds that
    // hash into every dependent. Measured 2026-08-25, appending a comment line
    // to the tracked `packages/ui/src/cn.ts`:
    //
    //     @appstrate/ui#build   21a3bd95da74576f → 8010b51e44ec9a69
    //     @appstrate/module-chat#build  218c98d62632f25a → 24043277209b5ba9
    //     @appstrate/web#build  6b772a1f1ff38d2c → 157c3743d72bfe52
    //
    // and every package's exported entrypoint lives under `src/` (checked
    // against each `exports` map), so `src/**` here covers what the bundler
    // reads. Do NOT add a `"$TURBO_ROOT$/packages/*/src/**"` glob on top: it
    // would make EVERY build task depend on EVERY package's sources, so one
    // package edit busts all of them, buying nothing. Re-run the probe above
    // before believing otherwise — `touch` moves no hash, turbo hashes content.
    "build": {
      "dependsOn": ["^build"],
      "inputs": [
        "src/**",
        "scripts/**",
        "public/**",
        "index.html",
        "vite.config.ts",
        "package.json",
        "tsconfig.json"
      ],
      "outputs": ["dist/**"]
    },
    "dev": {
      "dependsOn": ["^build"],
      "persistent": true,
      "cache": false
    },
    // Same defect `//#typecheck:scripts` documents below, one scope up: the
    // inputs must be what tsc COMPILES, not what the package is "about". Every
    // workspace script is `tsc --noEmit`, so the program is its tsconfig's
    // `include`, and workspaces reach past `src/` in every direction: apps/api
    // declares no `include` at all (so tsc takes the whole directory, test/
    // included), apps/cli and packages/core add `test` + `scripts`,
    // afps-runtime / afps-shared / mcp-transport / runner-pi add `test`, and
    // e2e compiles `**/*.ts` out of `tests/`, `fixtures/`, `helpers/`,
    // `pages/` and a root `playwright.config.ts`.
    //
    // Listing only `src/**` therefore let this task replay a cached PASS over
    // code it never compiled: a type error introduced in apps/api/test/**
    // alone moved no hash, turbo printed `Cached: 1`, and tsc never ran. `bun
    // test` does not close it either — bun executes tests without typechecking
    // them. Over-broad inputs cost one extra invalidation; under-broad inputs
    // are a silent green.
    //
    // `**/*.ts{,x}` is here so this list stops being a roster somebody has to
    // remember to extend. The enumeration above WAS the roster, and it failed
    // the first time it was tested: `e2e` got a `tsconfig.json` and a
    // `typecheck` script, nobody widened these inputs, and the task landed on
    // two inputs — `tsconfig.json` and `package.json` — so editing a spec left
    // its hash untouched and turbo replayed a stale green forever. A glob that
    // is a superset of any possible tsconfig `include` cannot rot that way.
    // The named directory globs stay for the non-TS assets a program can pull
    // in (JSON under `resolveJsonModule`, fixtures); they are no longer what
    // makes the TS itself visible.
    //
    // Not every workspace is served from here: `runtime-pi` and
    // `runtime-pi/sidecar` keep their own `turbo.json` overriding this task,
    // because runtime-pi's tsconfig EXCLUDES the nested sidecar workspace and
    // that negation (`!sidecar/**`) cannot be expressed in a shared list.
    // Reach for a package-level override only when you need that kind of
    // subtraction — otherwise widen this list.
    //
    // Probe when changing it: `turbo run typecheck --dry=json`, EDIT
    // apps/api/test/unit/rate-limit.test.ts (append a line — turbo hashes
    // content, so `touch` moves nothing and reads as a false negative), run
    // again — the hash must move.
    //
    // `!**/node_modules/**` for the same measured reason `//#lint` carries it,
    // and until now this task and that one stated contradictory beliefs about
    // the same hasher in the same file. Turbo does NOT skip gitignored files:
    // measured 2026-08-25, creating `apps/api/node_modules/.zzprobe/a.ts` moved
    // `@appstrate/api#typecheck` from `c22a75e9c2187f82` to `9b24b8cff0c07ef5`
    // — so `**/*.ts` reached into `node_modules` and every `bun install` busted
    // all 21 typecheck caches. That is cost rather than a false green (a busted
    // cache re-runs tsc), which is why it survived this long; it is still 21
    // needless tsc runs per install. `!**/dist/**` is deliberately NOT added:
    // `apps/cli` emits `dist/` and nothing typechecks it, but a package that
    // ships hand-written `.d.ts` under `dist/` would be silently dropped from
    // its own program's hash, and no such measurement has been made.
    "typecheck": {
      "dependsOn": ["^typecheck"],
      "inputs": [
        "src/**",
        "test/**",
        "scripts/**",
        "**/*.ts",
        "**/*.tsx",
        "!**/node_modules/**",
        "tsconfig.json",
        "tsconfig.*.json",
        "package.json"
      ]
    },
    // `lint` runs `scripts/lint.ts`, which hands eslint every TRACKED
    // `.ts/.tsx/.js/.jsx/.mjs/.cjs` file. These inputs only decide when turbo
    // REPLAYS it, so they must track that scope, not the other way round: a
    // zone eslint covers but this list omits is a zone whose findings arrive
    // once and are then cached away.
    //
    // Which is why this is a superset shaped like the script's own glob list
    // rather than a parallel directory roster. The roster this replaces
    // (`apps/*/src/**`, `packages/*/test/**`, …) had already fallen behind: it
    // did not reach `apps/web/vite.config.ts`, `packages/db/drizzle.config.ts`,
    // `packages/afps-runtime/bin/afps.ts` or either `examples/` file. Those
    // were outside eslint's `files` too, so the two lists were consistently
    // wrong together — the moment the config was widened to cover them, an
    // unwidened list here would have handed them one real run and then a cache
    // hit forever. Two supersets that cannot drift beats two rosters that
    // agreed by accident.
    //
    // The two negations are NOT decoration, and the comment they replace
    // claimed the opposite ("turbo hashes only non-ignored files, so `**` costs
    // nothing for `node_modules` / `dist`"). Measured 2026-08-25 on this
    // machine with a bare `**/*.{ts,tsx}`: creating `node_modules/.zzprobe/a.ts`
    // moved this task's hash `8be6968e1f982bc5` → `820658372368758f`. A
    // gitignored file DID enter the hash, so every `bun install` busted the
    // lint cache. With the negations in place the same probe leaves the hash
    // unmoved (`bfccf7e59fedff90` both times). Do not restore the claim
    // without re-running that probe.
    //
    // No `eslint.config.mjs` entry: `.mjs` is in the glob, so the config file
    // is already hashed — a rule change invalidates every verdict, derived
    // rather than restated. `package.json` is not matched by the glob and holds
    // the `lint` script itself, so it stays listed. `scripts/lint.ts`, which
    // now defines the scope, is matched by the glob like any other `.ts`.
    //
    // The glob restates the extension list that lives in TypeScript as
    // `SOURCE_GLOBS` in `scripts/lib/tracked-files.ts`. That restatement is the
    // one unavoidable copy — turbo reads JSON and cannot import it — so when
    // the extension list changes, change it in both places.
    //
    // The tsconfigs are listed because eslint READS them on every run and no
    // glob above reaches a `.json`: every `parserOptions.projectService` block
    // in the config resolves the nearest tsconfig for the files it covers, and
    // those tsconfigs set `target`, `lib`, `jsx`, `paths` and `allowJs`.
    // Without an entry, a PR editing one left this task's hash unmoved, turbo
    // replayed the cached logs, and the type-AWARE rules never re-derived a
    // single verdict — and `.github/workflows/check.yml` keys the restored
    // `.turbo/cache` on `bun.lock, eslint.config.mjs, .prettierrc, turbo.json,
    // tsconfig.base.json`, so via `restore-keys` that stale replay survived
    // across CI runs too. `//#verify:type-coverage` below already lists
    // `apps/web/tsconfig.json` in its own `inputs` for the same reason.
    // `apps/*/src/**/*.json` is the other half of the same tsconfig story. The
    // SPA's tsconfig sets `resolveJsonModule`, and 8 files under `apps/web/src`
    // import `../../locales/<lng>/<ns>.json`, so those JSON files are MEMBERS
    // of the type-aware program — while no glob above reaches a `.json`.
    // Measured 2026-08-25: adding a key to `apps/web/src/locales/fr/agents.json`
    // left this task's hash at `02fd25ac952d7956`, unmoved, so the one
    // type-aware rule in the config replayed its cached verdict over a program
    // whose contents had changed. Scoped to `apps/*/src` rather than
    // `**/*.json` on purpose: a repo-wide JSON glob would put every
    // `package.json`, every fixture and every generated artefact in the lint
    // cache key. 27 files today (17 api, 10 web).
    //
    // ─── The backend type-aware block (eslint.config.mjs) ────────────────
    //
    // `apps/api/src/**` and `packages/*/src/**` now carry three type-aware
    // rules (no-floating-promises, no-misused-promises, await-thenable), so
    // their programs are read on every run too, and the four entries below were
    // added for that. Each was PROVEN missing first — probe on 2026-09-08, on
    // the config as it stood, `turbo run '//#lint' --dry=json`, base hash
    // `ec64d50a6e1e20ab`, editing one file's CONTENT per row:
    //
    //   apps/api/tsconfig.json               ec64d50a6e1e20ab  UNMOVED
    //   packages/core/tsconfig.json          ec64d50a6e1e20ab  UNMOVED
    //   apps/api/src/data/featured-models.json  ec64d50a6e1e20ab  UNMOVED
    //   packages/core/schema/agent.schema.json  72a27df96ec0efbb  moved
    //
    // The last row is the interesting one, and it is why `packages/*/schema`
    // is listed anyway. It moved through `hashOfInternalDependencies`, not
    // through any glob here: the ROOT `package.json` depends on
    // `@appstrate/core` as `workspace:*`, and turbo hashes an internal
    // dependency by its publishable file set — core's `"files"` is
    // `["src", "schema", "LICENSE", "NOTICE"]`. That coverage is a
    // coincidence of two unrelated fields, it holds for only the five
    // packages the root happens to depend on, and `packages/core/tsconfig.json`
    // (same package, not in `files`) sits one row above showing it does NOT
    // generalise. Stated explicitly here instead of inherited.
    //
    // Same probe, same day, with the entries below in place — base hash
    // `cf20f4ec31c085ad`, and every row moves, including the two that already
    // worked:
    //
    //   apps/api/tsconfig.json                  77234cfc9d60ecfd
    //   packages/core/tsconfig.json             e21f6d8ca13a85fa
    //   apps/api/src/data/featured-models.json  369727613c425b9b
    //   packages/core/schema/agent.schema.json  574544fed5958af4
    //   apps/web/tsconfig.json                  bfac4ab8c5e96c95
    //   apps/web/src/locales/fr/agents.json     204f82518306970f
    //
    // (restoring every file returned the hash to `cf20f4ec31c085ad`).
    //
    // `apps/web/tsconfig.json` widened to `apps/*/tsconfig.json` and
    // `apps/web/src/**/*.json` to `apps/*/src/**/*.json` for the same reason
    // the eslint `files` roster was widened to a superset: the next app to get
    // a type-aware rule would otherwise get one real run and a cache hit
    // forever. Three app tsconfigs, sixteen package tsconfigs, four schema
    // JSONs today — all discovered by the glob, none listed.
    //
    // `tsconfig.base.json`, which every one of those extends, and `bun.lock`,
    // which decides which eslint plugin and which `bun-types` run, are both in
    // `globalDependencies`.
    "//#lint": {
      "inputs": [
        "**/*.{ts,tsx,js,jsx,mjs,cjs}",
        "!**/node_modules/**",
        "!**/dist/**",
        "package.json",
        "apps/*/tsconfig.json",
        "apps/*/src/**/*.json",
        "packages/*/tsconfig.json",
        "packages/*/schema/**/*.json",
        // The workspace manifests, for the same reason the tsconfigs above are
        // here: `exports`, `types` and `typesVersions` decide which `.d.ts` a
        // cross-workspace import resolves to, so editing one CAN change a
        // type-aware verdict while moving no `.ts` file. Unlike the four
        // entries above this was NOT reached through a measured false green —
        // it is a hole nobody probed, listed because an over-broad input costs
        // one extra invalidation and an under-broad one is a silent pass. The
        // same manifests are already inputs to `//#verify:type-coverage` below,
        // which measures the same programs.
        //
        // Probed 2026-09-08 with these two lines in place, appending a newline
        // to `packages/core/package.json` (content, not `touch`):
        //   base b5ff99806eeedafa → after c28441226f51236b → restored b5ff99806eeedafa
        "apps/*/package.json",
        "packages/*/package.json"
      ]
    },
    // No `inputs` on purpose. The root `format:check` script is now a bare
    // `prettier --check .`, whose scope is "every file in the repo minus
    // `.gitignore` + `.prettierignore`" — and that is exactly turbo's default
    // for a task on the `//` package. Any explicit list here could only be
    // narrower than what prettier checks, which is the failure mode this
    // comment used to warn about: a real drift replayed as a cached pass.
    "//#format:check": {},
    "//#verify:openapi": {
      "cache": false
    },
    "//#verify:compose-defaults": {
      "cache": false
    },
    // `cache: false`, like every sibling `verify:*` except type-coverage, and
    // that is the deliberate choice rather than an omission. This gate's
    // verdict depends on something turbo cannot hash: the git TAG namespace
    // (and `GITHUB_REF_NAME` on a tag push). A cached replay keyed on file
    // contents alone would report the previous verdict after a new release tag
    // appeared with no file having changed — the gate would go stale in exactly
    // the way the version it guards did. There is no `inputs` list that closes
    // that, so the task never caches and always re-reads the tags. It reads two
    // small file sets and spawns one `git tag`, so the cost is noise.
    "//#verify:release-version": {
      "cache": false
    },
    // The one new `verify:*` task that CAN cache correctly, so it does — its
    // verdict is a pure function of three file sets and nothing else (no git
    // refs, no clock, no network), which is exactly the condition
    // `//#verify:type-coverage` satisfies and every `cache: false` sibling does
    // not.
    //
    // The inputs ARE the gate's coverage, and a wrong list here is a false
    // GREEN, not a slow build — the incident class this file already documents
    // three times (`typecheck` replaying over uncompiled `test/**`, `//#lint`
    // replaying over an edited `apps/web/tsconfig.json`, `//#typecheck:scripts`
    // replaying over `knip.config.ts`). The three populations the script reads:
    //
    //   - `docs/ENV.md`               the rows
    //   - `packages/env/src/index.ts` the schema key set — the file, not the
    //                                 package: the script imports the module,
    //                                 and adding a key there with no row here
    //                                 must invalidate this verdict
    //   - `*.env.example`             both shipped example files. The glob is
    //                                 `**/.env.example` because the second one
    //                                 lives under `examples/self-hosting/`, and
    //                                 a root-anchored `.env.example` would miss
    //                                 it — the file list is discovered from the
    //                                 git index at runtime, so this glob must be
    //                                 a SUPERSET of what discovery can return.
    //
    // `**/` matching the ROOT file was the open question here, and it is the
    // one that decides whether this task caches a false GREEN: if `**/` needed
    // at least one directory segment, adding an undocumented variable to the
    // root `.env.example` would leave the hash unmoved and replay a cached
    // pass. MEASURED 2026-08-27 with the probe below rather than reasoned
    // about — turbo's `**/` DOES match at zero depth:
    //
    //     $ turbo run '//#verify:env-docs' --dry=json | jq '.tasks[0].inputs'
    //     { ".env.example": "4bcf3e5f…", "examples/self-hosting/.env.example": "6ff035f1…", … }
    //     # append a line to the ROOT .env.example, run again:
    //     task hash 7f43e544fa412d09 → 28829c1af54ed916   (moved)
    //
    // So both files are covered by the one entry and neither needs an explicit
    // root line — unlike `//#typecheck:scripts`, whose `knip.config.ts` and
    // `commitlint.config.ts` are named individually because no glob in that
    // list reaches them at all.
    //
    // `packages/module-*/src/env.ts` is the FOURTH population, added when the
    // gate stopped reading `packages/env` alone: a module declares environment
    // variables of its own and `docs/ENV.md` is now held to those too. It is a
    // glob rather than the one file that exists today for the same reason the
    // discovery in the script is — the next module must not need an edit here
    // to be covered, because the failure of forgetting one is a cached GREEN.
    //
    // `scripts/verify-env-docs.ts`, `scripts/lib/tracked-files.ts` and
    // `scripts/lib/module-env-schemas.ts` are the gate's own code; an edit to
    // any of them changes the verdict without touching a population above.
    //
    // Probe when changing it: `turbo run '//#verify:env-docs' --dry=json`, EDIT
    // one of these files (turbo hashes content — `touch` moves nothing and
    // reads as a false negative), run again — the hash must move.
    "//#verify:env-docs": {
      "inputs": [
        "docs/ENV.md",
        "packages/env/src/index.ts",
        "packages/module-*/src/env.ts",
        "**/.env.example",
        "scripts/verify-env-docs.ts",
        "scripts/lib/tracked-files.ts",
        "scripts/lib/module-env-schemas.ts",
        "package.json"
      ]
    },
    // `cache: false`, like most siblings: the run is a spawn of a cached
    // binary against 18 files and finishes in milliseconds, so a cache entry
    // would buy nothing and could only ever hide a finding.
    "//#verify:workflows": {
      "cache": false
    },
    "//#detect:breaking": {
      "cache": false
    },
    "//#verify:api-types": {
      "cache": false
    },
    // The one cached `verify:*` task — every other is `cache: false`, so this
    // is the only one where a stale replay is possible at all. Same defect as
    // `typecheck` and `//#typecheck:scripts`: `type-coverage --project
    // apps/web/tsconfig.json` measures the PROGRAM, and the workspace packages
    // `apps/web` imports are TypeScript source with no build step, so they are
    // in it. Measured contributor distribution at the time of writing: 705
    // `any` sites under `apps/web/src`, then 102 under `packages/core/src`, 55
    // under `packages/ui/src`, 53 under `packages/module-chat/src`, 9 under
    // `packages/afps-shared/src`. Listing only `apps/web/src/**` therefore let
    // an `any` introduced in `packages/core/src` drop the figure below the
    // `--at-least 98` floor while turbo replayed a cached PASS. `package.json`
    // is listed for symmetry with every other task here — the floor lives in
    // the script it holds; turbo already folds the root manifest into a `//`
    // task's hash on its own, so that entry documents rather than fixes.
    //
    // Probe when changing it: `turbo run '//#verify:type-coverage' --dry=json`,
    // EDIT packages/core/src/naming.ts (append a line — turbo hashes content,
    // so `touch` moves nothing and reads as a false negative), run again — the
    // hash must move.
    //
    // The script itself (root package.json) had a second, unrelated defect
    // worth recording here because this is where the task is documented:
    // `--ignore-files` resolves its pattern against the CWD, which for a root
    // script is the repo root, and the pattern read `src/api/schema.d.ts` —
    // a path that exists nowhere. Measured three ways: as written 99.14%
    // (106896/107817), with the flag DELETED ENTIRELY 99.14% (106896/107817,
    // byte-identical — the exclusion was doing nothing), and with the path
    // corrected to `apps/web/src/api/schema.d.ts` 99.04% (95538/96459). The
    // 11 328-identifier generated OpenAPI client is perfectly typed, so it
    // padded both sides of the ratio and every hand-written `any` was being
    // measured against an inflated denominator.
    //
    // The floor moved with it: `--at-least 98` was set against that inflated
    // figure and had never measured anything, so re-using it would have kept
    // ~1 000 identifiers of dead slack. It is now 99.
    //
    // How much slack that leaves, re-measured 2026-08-25 rather than estimated:
    // `bunx type-coverage --project apps/web/tsconfig.json --strict
    // --ignore-files "apps/web/src/api/schema.d.ts"` reports 95899/96819 =
    // 99.0498%. `--at-least 99` trips at 95851 typed identifiers, so the
    // headroom is 48 — the number of currently-typed identifiers that could
    // turn into `any` before this gate says no. (The comment this replaces said
    // "~40", which was a guess; the figure also moves with the denominator, so
    // re-run the command rather than trusting this line.) When it trips, type
    // the `any` that tripped it. Do not lower the floor; the whole point of
    // this pass was that a floor nobody re-derived is not a floor.
    "//#verify:type-coverage": {
      "inputs": [
        "apps/web/src/**",
        "apps/web/tsconfig.json",
        // `apps/web/tsconfig.json` now includes `vite.config.ts`, so it is in
        // the measured PROGRAM — and an input list that stops at `src/**` is
        // blind to it in exactly the way this comment block is about. Measured:
        // before this line, appending to `vite.config.ts` left this task's hash
        // byte-identical.
        "apps/web/vite.config.ts",
        "packages/*/src/**",
        "packages/*/package.json",
        "packages/*/tsconfig.json",
        "tsconfig.base.json",
        "package.json"
      ]
    },
    "//#build:system-packages:check": {
      "cache": false
    },
    "//#lint:manifest-casing": {
      "cache": false
    },
    "//#conformance:check": {
      "cache": false
    },
    "//#verify:module-isolation": {
      "cache": false
    },
    // `cache: false` for the reason every `verify:*` sibling carries it, plus
    // one of its own: this gate reads a module's newest drizzle SNAPSHOT to
    // learn which tables that module owns, and the snapshot it picks is chosen
    // at runtime from the journal. No `inputs` list names that file, so a
    // cached replay would check the module's SQL against a table set from
    // before its last migration.
    "//#verify:module-sql-boundary": {
      "cache": false
    },
    // `cache: false` like its `verify:*` siblings, and for the reason
    // `//#verify:release-version` spells out: this gate's file set is the git
    // INDEX, so `git add`ing a file changes the verdict. Nothing in an `inputs`
    // list expresses that, and a cached replay would report "licence boundary
    // clean" over a file it never read.
    "//#verify:license-boundary": {
      "cache": false
    },
    "//#verify:no-migration-dml": {
      "cache": false
    },
    "//#verify:module-contract": {
      "cache": false
    },
    "//#verify:dead-code": {
      "cache": false
    },
    // `scripts/tsconfig.json` is not an allowlist any more, and the files it
    // names are not the files it checks: several scripts (verify-openapi.ts,
    // generate-api-types.ts, detect-breaking-changes.ts) import
    // apps/api/src/openapi/, and tsc follows imports. The program is ~600
    // files — 350 under apps/api, most of the rest under packages/*.
    //
    // Inputs that listed only scripts/** therefore let this task replay a
    // cached PASS over code it never compiled, and CI restores the turbo cache
    // across runs: a PR breaking a symbol one of those scripts imports got
    // this gate for free. The list below is the program, not the entrypoints.
    //
    // Probe when changing it: `turbo run '//#typecheck:scripts' --dry=json`,
    // EDIT apps/api/src/index.ts (append a line — turbo hashes content, so
    // `touch` moves nothing and reads as a false negative), run again — the
    // hash must move.
    "//#typecheck:scripts": {
      "inputs": [
        "scripts/**/*.ts",
        "scripts/tsconfig.json",
        // The two root config files `scripts/tsconfig.json` pulls in. They are
        // in no other program, which is how `knip.config.ts` came to carry a
        // duplicate object key (TS1117) that nothing reported. Listing them in
        // the tsconfig alone does not close that: turbo hashes THIS list, so
        // without these two lines an edit to either file is a `cache hit,
        // replaying logs` and the gate reports a green it never re-derived.
        "knip.config.ts",
        "commitlint.config.ts",
        "apps/*/src/**",
        "apps/*/package.json",
        "apps/*/tsconfig.json",
        "packages/*/src/**",
        "packages/*/package.json",
        "packages/*/tsconfig.json",
        "tsconfig.base.json",
        "package.json"
      ]
    }
  }
}
